narrative:
## Managing Arc Lifecycles

Understanding how cloning and dropping affect Arc's reference count is key to avoiding memory leaks and use-after-free.

### Arc::clone — Increment

```xiom
let original = Arc.new(42);
// ref_count = 1

let copy1 = original.clone();
// ref_count = 2

let copy2 = original.clone();
// ref_count = 3
```

### Drop — Decrement

```xiom
let arc = Arc.new(Vec[Int]());
arc.push(1);
// ref_count = 1

{
  let clone = arc.clone();
  // ref_count = 2
}  // clone dropped — ref_count = 1

// arc still valid, ref_count = 1
mem.drop(arc);  // ref_count = 0 — Vec is freed
```

### The Last Handle

When ref_count hits 0, the inner value is dropped:

```xiom
fn create_and_drop() -> Int {
  let a = Arc.new(42);     // ref_count = 1
  let b = a.clone();       // ref_count = 2
  mem.drop(a);             // ref_count = 1 — value still alive
  mem.drop(b);             // ref_count = 0 — value dropped
  return 0;
}
```

### Preventing Leaks

```xiom
// Cycle = leak (Rc/Arc can't detect cycles)
// Let a = Arc.new(...);  // points to b
// Let b = Arc.new(...);  // points to a
// Both ref_counts never reach 0 — memory leak!
// Use Weak references to break cycles
```

### Monitoring

```xiom
let arc = Arc.new(0);
let before = arc.ref_count();
// do work...
let after = arc.ref_count();
// after == before means no leaks in that section
```

